================================================================================
PART 2b  CM211-Info-1: the line counts, and the method they are counted by
================================================================================
The supplement's PART D says the committed gen_regress.py is "763 lines" and the reduced one "733 lines". Counted by
newlines they are 762 and 732, and splitlines agrees. The file ends with a newline, so the newline count IS the line
count and my figures were each larger by one: an off-by-one in my method, not two defensible conventions. Both
probes now print the newline count, the splitlines count and whether the file ends with a newline, so every figure
carries its method.

TWO THINGS WORTH RECORDING BEYOND THE CORRECTION. First, the delta the section exists to prove is untouched: 762
minus 732 is the same 30 lines as 763 minus 733, and the hashes it turns on are unchanged, so the reconstruction
argument stands. Second, and this is why the row is worth more than the two digits, the SAME off-by-one sat in a
second probe of mine reporting gen_run.py as 514 lines where the newline count is 513, and I only found it because
fixing the first one made me check the other. I also fixed the wrong probe first: the cited 763 and 733 come from
the delta probe over gen_regress.py, and my first correction went to the probe over gen_run.py, which produces a
different pair of numbers entirely. So the lesson is not "count newlines" but "when a row cites a figure, find the
command that printed THAT figure before correcting anything".

================================================================================
PART 3  what still stands
================================================================================
Every red in the supplement discriminates, and none of the corrected numbers weakens that: each revert turns at
least one case BAD and every green restores the parent's own blob hashes. What changes is that the restore revert
is shown to break three cases rather than one, which is a stronger red than the file claimed, and that a reader can
now reproduce the edit exactly. The supplement's PART D reconstruction, its PART A loader command and its PART C
statement about the case with no red are untouched by this correction.

THE LESSON, recorded because it is a presentation rule and not a one-off: a retained log must not print a filtered
self-test excerpt without saying what the filter was, because a filtered count and a complete count of the same
artefact read as a contradiction. Either print the complete output or state the filter beside the excerpt.

